# Agent coding process

- One request = one measurable outcome. Stop when that outcome is verified.
- For work spanning more than three files, state a short plan before editing.
- Search narrowly first; read the smallest relevant set, normally 1–3 files in one layer.
- Do not scan generated files, caches, dependencies, binaries, model weights, uploads, or lockfiles unless the task requires them.
- Reuse information already established in the conversation; do not reopen unchanged files without a concrete reason.
- Prefer symbol/text search over reading entire large files. Read focused line ranges when possible.
- Before editing, identify the canonical implementation, its tests, and the nearest established pattern.
- Make the smallest coherent diff. No unrelated cleanup, renames, formatting sweeps, abstractions, or dependency upgrades.
- Extend existing interfaces and helpers before creating parallel stacks.
- Create a new file only when there is no natural existing home or the rule system requires focused separation.
- Do not generate unsolicited README, ADR, roadmap, example, migration, or test fixture files.
- Avoid speculative layers and generic frameworks for one use case.
- Keep responses concise: outcome, important decisions, verification, and blockers.
- Do not narrate routine reads/searches. Report only findings that change implementation or require a decision.
- Batch independent reads and checks. Run the narrowest useful test first, then broader checks proportional to risk.
- Never claim a test, migration, backup, security property, or deployment succeeded without evidence.
- If requirements conflict, security and data isolation win; surface the conflict instead of guessing.
- Ask only questions that materially alter schema, security, external side effects, destructive behavior, or architecture.
- Do not commit, push, deploy, migrate production, rotate secrets, or mutate external systems without explicit request.
- Preserve user changes. Never use destructive git commands to “clean” the workspace.
- Comments explain why and invariants, not obvious syntax. Delete dead code instead of commenting it out.
